    "taxonId": 322689,
    "summary": "A genus of perennial flowering herbs native to Africa. Its species form leafy basal rosettes and bear colourful daisy-like heads, while cultivated hybrids are widely grown as garden plants and cut flowers in many colours and forms.",
    "humanEdibility": "unknown",
    "edibilityNote": null,
    "evidence": {
      "summary": ["s1:p5", "s1:p12", "s1:p13"],
      "humanEdibility": []
    }
  },
  {
    "taxonId": 322946,
    "summary": "An East Asian genus of evergreen flowering perennials native to stream banks and seashores. Its plants spread through underground stems and have rounded, leathery leaves with bright yellow flowers in autumn and winter.",
    "humanEdibility": "unknown",
    "edibilityNote": null,
    "evidence": {
      "summary": ["s1:p1"],
      "humanEdibility": []
    }
  },
  {
    "taxonId": 323538,
    "summary": "A genus of flowering herbs and small shrubs, with most species endemic to Australia and a few occurring in New Zealand and New Guinea. They bear white, blue, pink or mauve ray flowers around yellow centres and inhabit environments ranging from rainy coasts and mountains to dry central Australia.",
    "humanEdibility": "unknown",
    "edibilityNote": null,
    "evidence": {
      "summary": ["s1:p1", "s1:p4", "s1:p6"],
      "humanEdibility": []
    }
  },
  {
    "taxonId": 323724,
    "summary": "An Australian shrub or small tree native to New South Wales and Queensland. It grows mainly in warm-temperate and subtropical rainforest gullies and along small watercourses, bearing large pointed leaves, yellowish flowers and small black berries containing many seeds.",
    "humanEdibility": "unknown",
    "edibilityNote": null,
    "evidence": {
      "summary": ["s1:p4", "s1:p6"],
      "humanEdibility": []
    }
  },
  {
    "taxonId": 323827,
    "summary": "An Australian-endemic small, rounded shrub with tangled, woolly branchlets that give it a silvery appearance. It grows on exposed southern coastal cliffs and dunes, tolerating wind, sea spray, drought and frost, and produces rounded white-yellow flower heads in summer.",
    "humanEdibility": "unknown",
    "edibilityNote": null,
    "evidence": {
      "summary": ["s1:p1", "s1:p3", "s1:p5"],
      "humanEdibility": []
    }
  },
  {
    "taxonId": 323942,
    "summary": "A tree fern native to southeastern mainland Australia, Tasmania and Norfolk Island. Its massive upright trunk supports a crown of long fronds that are dark green above and paler below, while old spiny frond bases often remain around the trunk after withering.",
    "humanEdibility": "yes",
    "edibilityNote": "An 1889 account says Aboriginal Australians ate the starchy pulp from the top of the trunk raw or roasted; it was also boiled.",
    "evidence": {
      "summary": ["s1:p1", "s1:p6", "s1:p7"],
      "humanEdibility": ["s1:p9"]
    }
  },
  {
    "taxonId": 324241,
    "summary": "A cosmopolitan genus of freshwater green algae, with no narrower native origin stated. It forms branching chains of cells enclosed in soft slime, usually growing in cold, well-aerated water attached to sand, rocks or other aquatic plants.",
    "humanEdibility": "unknown",
    "edibilityNote": null,
    "evidence": {
      "summary": ["s1:p2", "s1:p3", "s1:p6"],
      "humanEdibility": []
    }
  },
  {
    "taxonId": 325556,
    "summary": "A genus of unicellular freshwater green algae with no native origin stated. Its typically flattened cells have two matching, often intricately lobed halves joined by a narrow bridge containing the nucleus, and the algae occur mainly in nutrient-poor to moderately fertile lakes and bogs.",
    "humanEdibility": "unknown",
    "edibilityNote": null,
    "evidence": {
      "summary": ["s1:p1", "s1:p2", "s1:p5", "s1:p18"],
      "humanEdibility": []
    }
